WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
Linux fundamentals.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:05.962
Every container, every Kubernetes node, every cloud box.

3
00:00:05.962 --> 00:00:09.300
A small working set covers most of what you'll need.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.200
Everything lives in one tree.

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.900
Slash etc holds configuration.

6
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.367
Slash var slash log holds logs.

7
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:20.806
And slash proc exposes live process information.

8
00:00:20.806 --> 00:00:24.600
Everything is a file, so the same tools work on all of it.

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.633
Permissions come in three triplets.

10
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:29.858
The owner gets read and write.

11
00:00:29.858 --> 00:00:32.167
That's the six in chmod six hundred.

12
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:34.352
Group and other get nothing.

13
00:00:34.352 --> 00:00:35.900
Perfect for an ssh key.

14
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.467
Pipes are the superpower.

15
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.533
Grep keeps only the error lines.

16
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:42.267
Awk cuts out the message.

17
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.200
Sort groups identical ones.

18
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.700
Uniq counts them.

19
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.633
Sort again, biggest first.

20
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:49.533
And head shows the top five.

21
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:52.867
One more habit: kill is a request.

22
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:55.467
Dash fifteen asks the process politely.

23
00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:58.633
Dash nine is the last resort, with no cleanup.

24
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:00.967
Here's the toolkit.

25
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:05.633
Lsof shows who holds port eight thousand, and kill asks it to stop.

26
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.667
This one pipeline finds the five most common errors in a log.

27
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:16.733
Then chmod locks a key, and systemctl and journalctl check the service and tail its logs.

28
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:19.267
Here's a typical day.

29
00:01:19.567 --> 00:01:22.767
The staging deploy fails with address already in use.

30
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:31.367
You ssh in, lsof shows a zombie uvicorn from last week, kill clears it, and journalctl confirms a clean start.

31
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:37.020
Then one pipeline answers what's erroring, instead of downloading two gigabytes of logs.

32
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.967
Four minutes, all muscle memory.

33
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:40.933
Let's recap.

34
00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:44.000
One filesystem tree, where everything is a file.

35
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.133
Permissions are read, write and execute for owner, group and other.

36
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:50.833
Pipes chain small tools into big answers.

37
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:56.559
Prefer the polite kill, and use systemctl and journalctl for services.

38
00:01:56.559 --> 00:01:57.567
Next up: Git.
